## Finding packages

If you already know the name of the package you're interested in using, you can type it into the command line.

```{r}
#| eval: FALSE

install.packages("spatstat")
```

## Finding packages

You can also add packages from the **Tools** menu

![](InClassStatic/geosphere.png)

## Finding out about packages

R has been applied to a range of topics related to the environment, and there are numerous free tutorials and textbooks that can guide you through specifics in these areas. Some examples include:

- [A Primer of Ecology with R](https://hankstevens.github.io/Primer-of-Ecology/)

- [Using R for Introductory Econometrics](https://www.urfie.net/downloads/PDF/URfIE_web.pdf)

- [Introductory Biostatistics with R](https://tuos-bio-data-skills.github.io/intro-stats-book/)

- [R for Earth System Science](https://pjbartlein.github.io/REarthSysSci/index.html)

- [R for Social Science](https://r4np.com/)

## Finding out about packages

Many packages will come with reference manuals and "vignettes" that walk through the basics of how to use the package. For example:

- [`chirps`](https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/chirps/vignettes/Overview.html)

- [`patchwork`](https://patchwork.data-imaginist.com/)

- [plotluck](https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/plotluck/vignettes/plotluck.html)

## Quick Activity: Exploring packages

Search for one of the following packages:

- `adehabitatLT`

- `tidycensus`

- `carbonr`

- `stplanr`

See if you can find a reference manual, vignette, or a tutorial that demonstrates the use of this package.

